->array brute force
 first, we have to find the maximum. After finding the maximum,
we create an array with the maximum + 1 in length, and we loop the given array. 
For example, at index 2, the count of 2 will be stored. For example, let's say the maximum is 3.
The array size will be 3, and at index 2, the frequency will be stored as 3. 

class Solution {
    public boolean hasDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            max = Math.max(max, nums[i]);
        }

        int[] freq = new int[max + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            freq[nums[i]]++;
            if (freq[nums[i]] > 1) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}

java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index -100 out of bounds for length 1001
means your array contains negative numbers, and you're trying to use them as indices.


For example:
int[] nums = {5, -100, 10};
freq[nums[1]]++;   // freq[-100]++  ❌ Invalid index
Array indices in Java must be between 0 and length - 1.


class Solution {
    public boolean hasDuplicate(int[] nums) {
        HashSet<Integer>map = new HashSet<>();

        for(int a:nums){
            if(map.contains(a)){
                return true;
            }
            map.add(a);
        }
        return false;
        
    }
}
